course: biology
lesson: 97
title: The insect house: ants, stick insects, tarantulas and scorpions
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Above your head in the insect house a line of leafcutter ants carries green leaf pieces along a rope, and they are not lunch: the ants are farmers taking food to their fungus garden.
explain: Every tank holds a different survival trick: a stick insect sways like a twig, a scorpion glows under ultraviolet light, a cockroach hisses and a mantis swivels its head to stare at you.
explain: Counting legs sorts them out: ants, beetles, mantises and cockroaches have six legs and are insects, while tarantulas and scorpions have eight and are relatives of spiders.
explain: Cartoons get the numbers wrong: the ants in A Bug's Life walk on two legs and wave two arms, but a real ant has six legs, all fixed to the middle of its body.
explain: Some of the smallest animals here are the rarest of all, and zoos breed them to save kinds that would otherwise disappear.
fact: Leafcutter ants do not eat the leaves; they chew them into mush to grow a fungus garden and eat the fungus, and ants were farming fungus for millions of years before people farmed.
fact: Tiny leafcutter workers ride on the leaf pieces to guard the carriers from flies, and a big nest can hold millions of ants with a queen that lives over ten years.
fact: Stick insects look and sway like twigs to hide from birds that hunt by sight, and some can lay eggs that hatch without a male.
fact: Most tarantulas are not dangerous to people; their bite is like a bee sting, and some flick itchy hairs from their belly at predators; females can live 20 years or more.
fact: Scorpions glow blue-green under ultraviolet light because of a chemical in their outer shell; scientists are not sure why, and they find scorpions at night with UV torches.
fact: The honey bees that fly out to collect pollen and nectar are all female workers, and they do a waggle dance: its angle shows the direction of flowers compared with the sun and the length of the wiggle shows how far; Karl von Frisch won a Nobel Prize for this.
fact: Madagascar hissing cockroaches hiss by pushing air out of breathing holes on their sides called spiracles; insects breathe through these holes, not their mouths.
fact: A praying mantis can turn its head to follow moving prey before it strikes with spiky front legs, and tiny 3D glasses showed that mantises see depth.
fact: Dung beetles roll balls of dung to eat or lay eggs in, clearing and burying dung in farmers' fields, and some can steer at night by the Milky Way.
fact: The goliath and Hercules beetles are among the biggest insects; insects stay smallish partly because their breathing tubes work well only over short distances.
fact: Millions of years ago, when the air held more oxygen, some dragonfly relatives had wings about 70 centimetres across.
fact: Zoos breed rare small animals like Partula snails, nearly wiped out on their islands by one predator snail that people brought in, and the Lord Howe Island stick insect, which was thought extinct until a few were found on a sea rock in 2001.
fact: Millipedes are not insects; most have fewer than 400 legs, two pairs on most body parts, and one found in Australia in 2021 had over 1,300 legs.
exercise: What do leafcutter ants do with the leaves? | Grow a fungus garden to eat | Eat the leaves straight away | Build soft walls for their nest | Make soft beds for the queen to rest on
exercise: Why do tiny ants ride on the leaf pieces? | To guard the carriers from flies | Because they are too tired to walk | To steer the leaf like a boat | To eat the leaf on the way
exercise: How long can a leafcutter queen live? | Over ten years | About one week, like a fly | About one month or so | Over a hundred years
exercise: How does a stick insect hide from birds? | It looks and sways like a twig | It changes colour like a chameleon | It digs under the soil | It glows to scare them
exercise: Which is true of some stick insects? | Their eggs can hatch without a male | They lay all their eggs in ponds and water | They give birth to live babies | They never lay eggs
exercise: What is the bite of most tarantulas like for a person? | Like a bee sting | Deadly within minutes | Like a big dog's bite | Completely painless
exercise: How do some tarantulas defend themselves besides biting? | They flick itchy hairs from their belly | They spray ink | They roll up into a tight hairy ball | They hiss loudly
exercise: What happens to a scorpion under ultraviolet light? | It glows blue-green | It turns almost invisible | It falls fast asleep at once | It changes from black to red
exercise: What does the length of a bee's waggle dance show? | How far away the flowers are | How many flowers there are to visit | How sweet the nectar is | How old the bee is
exercise: Who won a Nobel Prize for discovering the bee dance? | Karl von Frisch | Charles Robert Darwin | Sir Isaac Newton | David Attenborough
exercise: How does a hissing cockroach hiss? | It pushes air out of holes on its sides | It rubs its back legs hard against its wings | It blows through its mouth | It clicks its jaws
exercise: What can a praying mantis do that most insects cannot? | Turn its head to look at you | Fly backwards | See in total darkness | Live underwater
exercise: How can some dung beetles steer at night? | By the Milky Way | By the smell of the dung | By the sound of frogs | By following each other
exercise: Why can't insects grow as big as dogs today? | Their breathing tubes work well only over short distances | They eat too little food | Their thin legs would snap in the wind like twigs | Birds eat all the big ones
exercise: How wide were the wings of some giant dragonfly relatives long ago? | About 70 centimetres | About 7 metres | About 7 centimetres | About 70 metres
exercise: Which rare animal was found on a sea rock in 2001 after being thought extinct? | The Lord Howe Island stick insect | The giant African goliath beetle | The hissing cockroach | The blue morpho
exercise: How many legs do most millipedes have? | Fewer than 400 | Exactly 1,000, as the name says | Exactly 100 on each side | Six, like insects
test: 5 leafcutter ants marching, 2 more ride on top. how many legs in total | 42 | 30 | 56 | 35
test: The tarantula tank and the scorpion tank each have one animal. How many legs altogether? | 16 | 12 | 14 | 20
test: In A Bug's Life, Flik the ant walks on two legs and uses two arms. How many legs does a real ant have? | Six | Four | Eight | Two
test: In Bee Movie, Barry is a boy bee who flies out to collect pollen. What is true of real honey bees? | The workers who collect pollen are all female | Only male bees collect pollen | Bees collect pollen with their little hands and fingers | The queen collects all the pollen
test: mate says that big hairy spider will kill you if it bites. true? | No, most tarantula bites are like a bee sting | Yes, every tarantula bite is deadly to people | Yes, but only at night | No, tarantulas have no fangs
test: Where are the holes a hissing cockroach breathes through? | Along its sides | At the very tip of each of its long feelers | Inside its mouth | Under its wings
test: Why does a stick insect sway even when there is no wind? | To look like a twig moving in a breeze | Because it is dizzy from hanging upside down | To keep warm | To shake off the dust
test: How is a leafcutter ant colony like a farm? | It grows a crop of fungus to eat | It keeps real cows and milks them in the nest | It plants trees in rows | It sells food to other ants
test: What would happen if the leafcutters' fungus garden died? | The colony would lose its food | The ants would eat the leaves instead | Nothing, ants do not need food | The queen would grow a new fungus from her wings
test: Why are dung beetles good friends to farmers? | They clear away and bury dung in the fields | They eat the slugs living on the farmers' crops | They frighten away crows | They pollinate the wheat
test: The keeper says the zoo saves rare snails. Why do tiny animals need saving too? | One invasive predator can wipe them out | They are too small to matter | They are kept only to feed the other animals | They cannot live outside zoos
test: Why is "thousand legs" a wrong name for most millipedes? | Most have fewer than 400 legs | They have exactly six legs | They have no legs at all | They all have exactly 100 legs
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which leafcutter ants convey fragments of leaves to their nest. | The cultivation of a fungus upon which the colony subsists | Direct consumption of the leaves by the workers | Construction of the walls and the roof of the underground nest | The bedding of the queen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the waggle dance of the honey bee? | Its angle denotes direction relative to the sun and its duration denotes distance | It is performed solely to attract a mate | It serves to warm the hive in winter | It denotes the precise number of bees required to gather the nectar
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of legs possessed by three insects together with two scorpions. | Thirty-four | Thirty | Twenty-eight | Forty
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the respiration of insects. | They breathe through openings along their sides termed spiracles | They breathe through their mouths by means of a pair of small lungs | They breathe through gills in the abdomen | They do not require oxygen
